dio.pause\\\", \\\"game.hook('input',()=>{peerNavigation();const inDev=['dev','devStage','snapshot'].includes(game.shell.screen);if(wasDev&&\"'!inDev){devScale=1;game.timeScale=1;game.live=!!session?.active;wasDev=false;}audio.pause\")\nopen(p,'\"'w').write(s)\np='src/render.js';s=open(p).read().replace(\\\"this.clock+=dt;\\\", \\\"this.clock+=dt*(this.game.reduceMotion?.2:1);\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"const [x,y]=r.w(f.x||0,f.y||0,0),u=f.life;\\\", \\\"const [x,y]=r.w(f.x||0,f.y||0,0),u=f.life,motion=g?.reduceMotion?.25:1;\\\")\n# world method has no g local; use this.game in custom effect path.\ns=s.replace(\\\"motion=g?.reduceMotion?.25:1\\\", \\\"motion=this.game.reduceMotion?.25:1\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"P.blend(ctx,1-u/.35,'add'\\\", \\\"P.blend(ctx,(1-u/.35)*Math.max(.18,this.game.settings.flashes)*(this.game.reduceMotion?.65:1),'add'\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"R=u*26*z;\\\", \\\"R=u*26*z*motion;\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"ring(ctx,x,y,(8+u*19)*z\\\", \\\"ring(ctx,x,y,(8+u*19*motion)*z\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"for(let i=0;i<18;i++){\\\", \\\"for(let i=0;i<(this.game.reduceMotion?6:18);i++){\\\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\nnode --check src/main.js\nnode --check src/render.js\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"The expanded browser suite passed 157 checks, including rebinding, controller stubs, touch gestures, attract mode, developer tools, and five window shapes. It caught a couch-control conflict and two phone layout overlaps, which are fixed. A real Hub run also reached the ending and preserved the same team result on both clients.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"command -v biome; head -14 research.md; head -20 vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js; git -C vendor/engine-repo ls-tree --name-only HEAD | rg 'LICENSE|NOTICE|COPYING'; git -C vendor/hub-repo ls-tree --name-only HEAD | rg 'LICENSE|NOTICE|COPYING'; ls evidence\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"command -v biome; head -14 research.md; head -20 vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js; git -C vendor/engine-repo ls-tree --name-only HEAD | rg 'LICENSE|NOTICE|COPYING'; git -C vendor/hub-repo ls-tree --name-only HEAD | rg 'LICENSE|NOTICE|COPYING'; ls evidence\"","aggregated_output":"/home/micha/.local/bin/biome\n# Evidence ledger\n\nInspected 2026-11-06. These are source checks, not claims that the original game was played here.\n\n| Reference | Actually inspected | What it supports and uncertainties |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Original U.S. manual](https://odyssey2.info/library/manuals/us_killerbees/Killer%20Bees%20(USA).pdf) | Extracted text of the twelve-page PDF, particularly printed rules pages 3–9. | Twelve starting bees; contact slows and eventually defeats Beebots; interrupted contact lets them recover; red/blue robots turn differently; graves obstruct robots. Hostile swarms mature through three behaviors. The horizontal ray removes rival swarms, including touching ones, recharges on a robot kill, and cannot stack charges. Survival affects the round bonus. Exact sound and animation timing are not established by text. |\n| [Bob Harris interview, Classic Gamer 5, p.40](https://www.oldschoolgamermagazine.com/osgdigital/cgm05/files/basic-html/page40.html) | HTML transcription of page 40. | Creator describes twelve individual bees, patterned robot turns, useful grave placement, entrance warnings, and the increasing survival bonus. His recollection says a Voice-module effect inspired the buzzing concept. This is retrospective testimony; no original sound was heard. |\n| [The Fantastic, Fantastic World of Bob](https://experienciaodyssey.com.br/the-fantastic-fantastic-world-of-bob/) | Creator interview text, especially arena backgrounds, robot movement, and enemy entrances. | Describes the readability problem with decorated arena backgrounds, gradual individual losses, predictable turns, and moving arrivals into visible cells after an unfair spawn death. Some recollections are explicitly uncertain. |\n| [Recording without The Voice](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i3OVu6cs3CQ), [with The Voice](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yN8ekDBvpJY) | URLs supplied in PLAN.md; neither viewed nor listened to. | Original pitch, envelopes, speech wording, and audiovisual timing remain **unknown**. This game's score, effects, and gibberish are original code compositions. |\n| [Required engine handoff](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff) | Vendored README, API.md, settings and ChipAudio implementation; renderer/input read by their respective owners. | my-3D2dge v0.89.1 at commit `8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691`. Native overhead view, fixed 120 Hz stepping, custom rigs, persistent settings, chip instruments, native music layers, and separate SFX/music/voice buses. No external audio files are necessary. |\n| [AI Hub SDK and harness](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub) | Network owner inspected the full README, pristine session client, shared-world arena example, harness conformance logic, and official checker. | Session Protocol 1, client 1.4.0, commit `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`. Metadata schema, start-clock gating, dedicated authority without a local seat, lifecycle/results, and transport limits come from the actual vendored contract. Local WebRTC signalling and gameplay are implemented separately; arbitrary internet/NAT reachability remains unknown without TURN. |\n\n## Deliberate additions\n/*!\n * my-3D2dge v0.89.1  (My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine)\n * Procedural pixel-puppet engine for every 2D perspective.\n * https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge  (MIT License)\n *\n * READ THIS FIRST (humans and AI models extending a my-3D2dge game)\n * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\n * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\n *    Game logic never thinks about screen pixels.\n * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\n *    re-renders from another angle: E.VIEWS.iso, .threequarter, .topdown,\n *    .brawler, .side  (or build your own: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)).\n * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\n *    joints from math every step, in 3D, then project them. That is why they move\n *    fluidly and can face any angle in any view without extra art. Small things\n *    (coins, bullets, icons) can be pixel sprites written as strings: E.sprite().\n * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\n *    snaps to whole pixels. Never use ctx.arc / ctx.stroke / ctx.fillText on the game\n *    buffer: they anti-alias and break the look. Translucency and glow go through\nLICENSE\nabout-browser.png\naudio-results.json\nbrowser-results.json\ndirect-foundation.json\nhub-host-leave-2.json\nhub-natural-victory.json\nhub-server-2.json\nhub-standard-1.json\nhub-standard-2.json\nnative-results.json\nnetwork-direct-results.json\nnetwork-natural-results.json\nnetwork-results.json\npause-browser.png\nplay-first.png\nplay-fixed.png\nportrait-chromium.png\nportrait-webkit.png\nsimulation.json\ntitle-browser.png\ntitle-first.png\ntitle-fixed.png\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
